\b 3. README overview\b0\par
\b Architecture\b0\par
Darukaa.Earth is a full-stack geospatial carbon and biodiversity intelligence platform. The frontend uses React, Vite, and TypeScript. The backend uses FastAPI, SQLAlchemy, and Alembic. Docker Compose runs the frontend, backend, and PostgreSQL/PostGIS database services. GIS support is designed around Mapbox GL JS, Mapbox Draw, GeoAlchemy2, and GeoJSON workflows.\par
\par
\b Database and schema\b0\par
PostgreSQL 16 with PostGIS stores production data. SQLAlchemy models define users, projects, sites, and related application data. Alembic migrations are located in backend/migrations/versions, including the initial schema and native PostGIS geometry migration. The test path can use SQLite and automatically bootstraps tables.\par
\par
\b Local setup\b0\par
1. Clone the repository.\par
2. Copy .env.example to .env and replace placeholder secrets and provider tokens as needed.\par
3. Run docker compose up --build from the repository root.\par
4. For manual backend setup, run alembic upgrade head from backend, then start the FastAPI application.\par
5. From frontend, install dependencies with npm install and use npm run dev.\par
6. Run backend tests with pytest. Run frontend checks with npm run lint and npm run build.\par
\par
\b CI/CD\b0\par
The repository is structured for GitHub Actions, but no workflow files are currently present. Deployment targets described by the project are Vercel for the frontend and Render or Railway-compatible hosting for the backend. CI/CD workflows for linting, testing, and deployment remain to be added.\par
\par
\b Reviewer notes\b0\par
No hosted credentials are included. Local Docker Compose uses the development database values darukaa/darukaa for the darukaa database only. The .env.example file contains placeholders for JWT_SECRET_KEY, MAPBOX_ACCESS_TOKEN, and AI_API_KEY; replace them locally and do not commit real secrets.\par
\par
The current repository README contains the longer project overview, feature list, migration details, testing commands, limitations, and future enhancements.\par
